research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-nth-term-test.md-789-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-nth-term-test.md-790-- **Two indices, doing different work.** The index $k$ runs over the terms and is
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-nth-term-test.md-791-  bound; the index $n$ runs over the partial sums and is the variable in which the
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-nth-term-test.md-792-  limit is taken. Confusing them is the commonest slip in the subject, and it is
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-nth-term-test.md-793-  the reason the definition above fixes $s_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$ rather than
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-nth-term-test.md-794-  $\sum_{k \le n} a_k$: with this choice the recursion $s_{n+1} = s_n + a_n$ is
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/lem-nth-term-test.md-795-  the one supplied by [[def-finite-sum]], with no shift anywhere.
