research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1311-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1312-## Facts & Assumptions
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1313-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1314-**Given:** A sequence $(a_k)$ of reals whose series converges, with partial sums $s_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$ ([[def-series]], [[def-finite-sum]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1315-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1316-[A1] $(s_n)$ converges to some real $S$ ([[def-series]], [[def-real-limit]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1317-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1318-[L1] The recursion clause of the finite sum: $s_{n+1} = s_n + a_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-finite-sum]], [[def-series]]).
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1319-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1320-[L2] Convergence depends only on the tail: if $(x_n)$ converges to $x$ then so does its $1$-st tail $(x_{n+1})_{n}$ ([[lem-limit-of-tail]], [[def-sequence]]).
--
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1328-1.1 The sequence $(s_{n+1})_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is the $1$-st tail of $(s_n)$, and it converges to $S$. [A1, L2]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1329-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1330-1.2 From the recursion clause, $a_n = s_{n+1} - s_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$. [L1, algebra]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1331-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1332-2.1 Hence $(a_n)$ is the difference of two sequences converging to $S$, so it converges to $S - S = 0$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, A1, L3]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1333-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1334-3.1 That is the claim, and its contrapositive is the assertion that a series whose terms do not tend to $0$ diverges. [step 2.1] ∎
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1335-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1336-## Remarks
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1337-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md:1338:- **This is a necessary condition and never a sufficient one.** It rules a series out; it never rules one in. The harmonic series has terms tending to $0$ and diverges, which is exactly the content of [[fs-nth-term-test-converse]].
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1339-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/cex-condensation-needs-monotonicity.md-1340-- **What the proof actually uses.** Only that the partial sums converge and that consecutive partial sums differ by a term. No sign hypothesis is placed on $(a_k)$, and none is available at this point on the page.
