research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-249-      "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-250-      "quote": "Let $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ be sequences of reals and suppose there is\n$K \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with\n\n$$0 \\;\\le\\; a_k \\;\\le\\; b_k \\qquad \\text{for all } k \\ge K .$$\n\nThen:\n\n1. if $\\sum b_k$ converges then $\\sum a_k$ converges ([[def-series]]);\n2. if $\\sum a_k$ diverges then $\\sum b_k$ diverges.\n\nThe same statement holds verbatim for series with a general starting index $m$,\napplied to the shifted sequences of [[def-series]].\n\nThe hypothesis is on the terms from some index on, not on all of them: finitely\nmany terms of either sequence may violate it, or be negative, without affecting\nthe conclusion. What may not be dropped is nonnegativity of $(a_k)$ from that\nindex on.",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-251-      "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-252-        "6.1"
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-253-      ]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-254-    },
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-255-    {
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-256-      "fact": "L7",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-257-      "source": "lem-nth-term-test",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-258-      "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md:259:      "quote": "Let $(a_k)$ be a sequence of reals. If the series $\\sum a_k$ converges\n([[def-series]]) then the sequence $(a_k)$ converges ([[def-real-limit]]), with\n\n$$\\lim_{k} a_k \\;=\\; 0 .$$\n\nEquivalently, in the contrapositive form in which the lemma is almost always\nused: **if $(a_k)$ does not converge to $0$ then $\\sum a_k$ diverges.**\n\nThe same statement holds for a series $\\sum_{k \\ge m} a_k$ with a general\nstarting index, with the conclusion that $(a_{j+m})_{j \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ converges\nto $0$: that series is by definition the series of the sequence\n$b_j = a_{j+m}$ ([[def-series]]), so it is the statement above applied to $(b_j)$.\n\nThe converse is false; it is refuted by [[fs-nth-term-test-converse]].",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-260-      "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-261-        "6.2",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-262-        "7.1"
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-263-      ]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-264-    },
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-265-    {
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-266-      "fact": "L7",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-267-      "source": "def-real-limit",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-268-      "source_section": "Definition",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-ratio-test.md-269-      "quote": "A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals **converges** to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ when for\nevery rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with\n$|x_k - x| < \\hat\\varepsilon$ for all $k \\ge K$. It is **Cauchy** when for\nevery rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with\n$|x_k - x_l| < \\hat\\varepsilon$ for all $k, l \\ge K$.",
--
