research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-292-      "fact": "L7",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-293-      "source": "lem-nth-term-test",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-294-      "source_section": "Statement",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md:295:      "quote": "Let $(a_k)$ be a sequence of reals. If the series $\\sum a_k$ converges\n([[def-series]]) then the sequence $(a_k)$ converges ([[def-real-limit]]), with\n\n$$\\lim_{k} a_k \\;=\\; 0 .$$\n\nEquivalently, in the contrapositive form in which the lemma is almost always\nused: **if $(a_k)$ does not converge to $0$ then $\\sum a_k$ diverges.**\n\nThe same statement holds for a series $\\sum_{k \\ge m} a_k$ with a general\nstarting index, with the conclusion that $(a_{j+m})_{j \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ converges\nto $0$: that series is by definition the series of the sequence\n$b_j = a_{j+m}$ ([[def-series]]), so it is the statement above applied to $(b_j)$.\n\nThe converse is false; it is refuted by [[fs-nth-term-test-converse]].",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-296-      "uses": [
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-297-        "5.2"
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-298-      ]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-299-    },
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-300-    {
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-301-      "fact": "L7",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-302-      "source": "def-real-limit",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-303-      "source_section": "Definition",
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/thm-root-test.md-304-      "quote": "A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals **converges** to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ when for\nevery rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with\n$|x_k - x| < \\hat\\varepsilon$ for all $k \\ge K$. It is **Cauchy** when for\nevery rational $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K$ with\n$|x_k - x_l| < \\hat\\varepsilon$ for all $k, l \\ge K$.",
