research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-146-[L2] Convergence depends only on the tail: if $(x_n)$ converges to $x$ then so does its $1$-st tail $(x_{n+1})_{n}$ ([[lem-limit-of-tail]], [[def-sequence]]).
--
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-154-1.1 The sequence $(s_{n+1})_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is the $1$-st tail of $(s_n)$, and it converges to $S$. [A1, L2]
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-155-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-156-1.2 From the recursion clause, $a_n = s_{n+1} - s_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$. [L1, algebra]
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-157-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-158-2.1 Hence $(a_n)$ is the difference of two sequences converging to $S$, so it converges to $S - S = 0$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, A1, L3]
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-159-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-160-3.1 That is the claim, and its contrapositive is the assertion that a series whose terms do not tend to $0$ diverges. [step 2.1] ∎
