research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-163-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md:164:- **This is a necessary condition and never a sufficient one.** It rules a series out; it never rules one in. The harmonic series has terms tending to $0$ and diverges, which is exactly the content of [[fs-nth-term-test-converse]].
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-165-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/lem-nth-term-test--f5066f5fbfe8d998.prompt.md-166-- **What the proof actually uses.** Only that the partial sums converge and that consecutive partial sums differ by a term. No sign hypothesis is placed on $(a_k)$, and none is available at this point on the page.
