research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1067-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1068-**Given:** A sequence $(a_k)$ of reals whose series converges, with partial sums $s_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$ ([[def-series]], [[def-finite-sum]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1069-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1070-[A1] $(s_n)$ converges to some real $S$ ([[def-series]], [[def-real-limit]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1071-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1072-[L1] The recursion clause of the finite sum: $s_{n+1} = s_n + a_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-finite-sum]], [[def-series]]).
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1073-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-retry27-fs-nth-term-test-converse.prompt.md-1074-[L2] Convergence depends only on the tail: if $(x_n)$ converges to $x$ then so does its $1$-st tail $(x_{n+1})_{n}$ ([[lem-limit-of-tail]], [[def-sequence]]).
