research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624327-+{"id":"thm-p-series-rational","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf","https://math.rice.edu/~semmes/math331.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.21 and Semmes's condensation chapter give the standard iff threshold p>1 for positive real exponents. The item restricts to rational p because that is the power operation available at this reading level and formalises canonical naturals, so it is a semantic specialization. The proof is the standard condensation-to-geometric-series argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624328-+{"id":"thm-root-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Theorem 4.27 states the limsup root test with absolute convergence below 1 and divergence above 1; the surrounding text gives both outcomes at equality. The item merely reindexes |a_n|^(1/n) as |a_{k+1}|^(1/(k+1)) to avoid exponent 1/0. Its proof follows the sourced geometric comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624329-+{"id":"thm-ratio-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests"],"rationale":"The source states the limsup/liminf ratio test exactly: limsup below 1 gives absolute convergence and liminf above 1 gives divergence, with the intervening regime inconclusive. The item makes nonvanishing and the index-shift convention explicit. Its proof is the standard eventual geometric comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624330-+{"id":"cor-root-test-dominates-ratio-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html"],"rationale":"The CSUDH notes give the full ratio-root liminf/limsup chain, state that the root test is strictly stronger, and use the same sequence 2^{(-1)^n-n} as witness. The item shifts indices and expands both convergence and divergence translations through local test statements. Its proof follows the sourced inequality-and-witness argument.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624331-+{"id":"thm-kummer-test","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Thomson–Bruckner–Bruckner Theorem 3.37 gives exactly the positive-weight Kummer expression, the positive-liminf convergence criterion, and the eventual-nonpositive plus divergent reciprocal-weight criterion. The proof follows the sourced telescoping comparison, expanded with extended-real tail-infimum details.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624332-+{"id":"cor-ratio-test-is-kummer-with-constant-weights","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"Both textbooks explicitly identify d'Alembert's ratio test as Kummer's test with constant weights. The item states only the two limsup/liminf ratio conclusions recovered in the positive-term setting and proves them through reciprocal bounds, so it is an expanded semantic specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624333-+{"id":"cor-raabe-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state Raabe's positive-term test and identify it as Kummer with harmonic weights. The item uses k+1 rather than k because its naturals start at zero, and uses liminf/limsup rather than assuming a single limit. The proof is the sourced Kummer specialization with that convention delta.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624334-+{"id":"thm-gauss-test","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convergence_tests"],"rationale":"The sources state Gauss's test for a_n/a_{n+1}=1+alpha/n+O(n^{-beta}) with beta>1, convergent exactly for alpha>1. The item's bound |r_k|<=C k^{-1-epsilon}, epsilon positive rational, is the same big-O regime after choosing beta=1+epsilon, with local rational-power conventions. Its borderline proof is a locally constructed summable-error product argument rather than the sourced logarithmic/Kummer proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-624335-+{"id":"thm-abel-dini","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The source states the divergent-series Abel–Dini–Pringsheim theorem for positive terms with inclusive partial sums S_n: sum a_n/S_n diverges and sum a_n/S_n^t converges for t>1, hence in particular at t=2. These are exactly the item's two claims and convention. The local proof derives the two estimates directly from Cauchy and telescoping facts.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
