research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787515-+{"id":"fs-universal-comparison-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem"],"rationale":"The Abel–Dini–Pringsheim theorem explicitly constructs, from any divergent positive series, another divergent positive series with smaller terms; this rules out a slowest divergent series and any single universal comparison series. The item presents the negation as a false claim and follows the sourced construction b_n/B_n with local quantifiers made explicit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787516-+{"id":"ex-harmonic-series-diverges","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.11 states that the harmonic series diverges and gives the same powers-of-two block argument and logarithmic-rate lower bounds. The item adds a condensation proof and formalises the explicit lower bound in local finite-sum notation; its Oresme block proof follows the source.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787517-+{"id":"ex-sum-of-reciprocal-squares-converges","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.20 proves convergence of sum 1/n^2 by the same comparison 1/(n+1)^2<1/(n(n+1)) and obtains the upper bound 2. The item shifts that calculation to k>=2 and states the safe non-strict bound at most 2. Its verification follows the sourced telescoping comparison.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787518-+{"id":"ex-geometric-series-computed","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter gives the geometric sum formula and its starting-index convention. The two values 1 for sum_{k>=1}2^{-k} and 3/4 for sum_{k>=0}(-1/3)^k are direct specializations packaged together by the item. Their local arithmetic verification is not a traced source proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787519-+{"id":"ex-telescoping-sum-computed","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"Hunter Example 4.5 gives exactly sum_{k>=1}1/(k(k+1))=1 through the identity 1/(k(k+1))=1/k-1/(k+1). The item's verification is the same telescoping calculation with local dependency citations.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787520-+{"id":"ex-condensation-applied","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.rice.edu/~semmes/math331.pdf","https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources apply condensation to the p-series and obtain the geometric ratio 2^{1-p}, with the threshold p>1. The item restricts to rational exponents, displays the algebra separately, and tabulates p=1/2,1,2. Its verification follows the sourced calculation under local rational-power notation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787521-+{"id":"ex-abel-dini-pair-for-the-harmonic-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abel%E2%80%93Dini%E2%80%93Pringsheim_theorem","https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"The Abel–Dini source explicitly applies the theorem to the harmonic series and records the logarithmically corrected family, while Hunter supplies the harmonic divergence. The item uses exact inclusive harmonic sums and selects exponents 1 and 2. The direct dependency application is locally written; its inaccurate 'no last divergent series in the family' sentence is an A2 defect.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787522-+{"id":"ex-raabe-decides-where-ratio-fails","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://people.math.sc.edu/girardi/m5545/TBB-AllChapters-Landscape.pdf","https://people.math.binghamton.edu/dikran/478/Ch5.pdf","https://www.math.ucdavis.edu/~hunter/intro_analysis_pdf/intro_analysis.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state that Raabe decides positive series whose ordinary ratio tends to 1 and give the p-series as the governing family. The item specializes to p=2 and computes R_k=2+1/(k+1) exactly, while the ratio tends to 1. This particular worked verification is locally composed.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-real-analysis-series.log-787523-+{"id":"cex-ratio-inconclusive-root-decides","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.csudh.edu/~pong/a3/Notes_01.html"],"rationale":"The CSUDH notes use the same sequence 2^{(-1)^n-n}, compute alternating ratios 1/8 and 2, and obtain root limit 1/2 to show strict root-test dominance. The item translates that standard witness into the library's alternating-sequence interface and cites the earlier computation; its proof is an adapted sourced verification.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
